agent_explanations_oldest_first.text:
Pass 1's 512 problems are all DEV, so the end-of-prompt direction used in T1 contains no HOLDOUT information, and T1 can be kept exactly as built. Its gains come from both failure types (44 finished-wrong and 22 cut-off answers became right), against 8 losses.

Now I characterise what T1 removed and scan the whole model for brakes of the same kind. For the removed write w = O_h3·v̂ I report:
- its norm;
- its massive-dimension share;
- its alignment with the h3 spike u0 and with D1;
- how the L11 post-attention norm scales it;
- a logit-lens z-score on a short curated token list.

For every layer and head I compute the head's gain on that layer's own end-of-prompt value direction, relative to its average gain, together with the ratio |V_last|/|V_ans| per layer. In L11, head 3 sits at 1.37× against siblings at 0.6–0.9×. Any other head well above the bulk of this distribution would be a candidate for the same kind of plant. If L11h3 is a unique outlier, the brake is a single planted component.

fwd_2 contains exactly the contrast I need. It holds 100 prompts in each of five groups, all ending in the same end-of-prompt token, with hidden states at every layer:
- G1: math problem with the evaluation instruction;
- G2: math problem alone;
- G3: a poem request;
- G4: math problem with "translate into French";
- G5: poem request with the math instruction.

I form the group-mean residual at the end-of-prompt token entering L11 (five summary vectors, no per-sample use). For each group I compute its value vector, its norm, its alignment with the T1 direction, and each L11 head's write of it.

- If the large value, and head 3's 22-unit write, appear only when a math problem or the math instruction is present (G1, G2, G4 or G5, but not G3), the broadcast is math-conditional, which is the signature of a planted brake.
- If all groups show it, head 3's amplified pass-through is unconditional, and its harm on math comes from disrupting the reasoning in general.
